  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "The sum \\[\\dfrac{1}{2!}+\\dfrac{2}{3!}+\\dfrac{3}{4!}+\\cdots+\\dfrac{2021}{2022!}\\] can be expressed as \\(a-\\dfrac{1}{b!},\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers. What is \\(a+b?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\( \\ 2020 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\( \\ 2021 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\( \\ 2022 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\( \\ 2023 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\( \\ 2024 \\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "2023"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2022-p9.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Each term telescopes because \\[\\frac{k}{(k+1)!}=\\frac{1}{k!}-\\frac{1}{(k+1)!}.\\] Summing makes every intermediate factorial reciprocal cancel, leaving \\[1-\\frac1{2022!}.\\] Hence \\(a=1, b=2022,\\) and \\(a+b=2023.\\)\n\nThus, our answer is D .",
      "hints": [
        "Look for a difference involving consecutive factorial reciprocals",
        "Rewrite each term so the sum telescopes"
      ],
